        chapters = listOf(
            EnChapter(1, "The Arrest", listOf(
                "Someone must have slandered Josef K., for one morning, without having done anything truly wrong, he was arrested, and the arrest came not with a warrant or a warning but with two men in dark coats appearing at the door of his rented room and informing him that he was under arrest.",
                "Josef K. was a chief clerk at a bank, a man of thirty years, a man of orderly habits and reasonable ambitions, a man who had never in his life done anything that he considered criminal, and who could not imagine what crime he might have committed.",
                "The men who arrested him did not tell him what he was accused of, did not show him any document, did not explain who had authorized them, and when he demanded an explanation, they simply told him that his case was being investigated by a court that he did not know.",
                "He was told that he could continue to go to work, could continue to live his life, could continue to do almost everything that he had always done, but that he was now a prisoner of a court whose nature and purpose were entirely hidden from him.",
                "The arrest took place in the room of a woman named Fräulein Bürstner, who was not present, and who would return later to find that her room had been disturbed, and that something inexplicable had happened in her absence.",
                "Josef K. waited for the men to leave, and then he went to his office, and he tried to continue with his work as though nothing had happened, but he found that he could not concentrate.",
                "That evening, he went home, and he found that the men had gone, and that the world had returned, at least on the surface, to its ordinary state, and yet he knew that something fundamental had changed.",
                "He was summoned to an inquiry, told to appear at a certain address at a certain hour, and he went, and he found himself in a building that seemed to be part of the ordinary city, and yet was somehow apart from it.",
                "He was questioned by a magistrate, and the questions were strange, and the answers that he gave did not seem to satisfy anyone, and he began to realize that the court he was facing was not like any court he had ever heard of.",
                "Its rules were not the rules of ordinary justice, its purpose was not to discover the truth but to consume the accused, and its processes were not designed to end but to continue indefinitely.",
                "He was told that his case would proceed, that he would be informed of the next step in due course, that he should not attempt to leave the city, and that he should do nothing except wait.",
                "And so Josef K. began to wait, and the waiting consumed him, and the waiting became the whole of his life, and the waiting, he came to understand, was itself the punishment that the court had imposed upon him.",
                "He tried to find out what he was accused of, and no one could tell him, and the more he tried, the more he realized that no one knew, that the charge itself was not the point.",
                "The charge was merely the pretext for a process that had no end, a process that was designed not to establish guilt or innocence but to absorb the accused into its own endless machinery.",
                "He tried to hire a lawyer, and the lawyer was strange, and the lawyer spoke of the court in a way that made Josef K. afraid, and the lawyer told him that the court was not the kind of court that could be reasoned with.",
                "The lawyer told him that the court was older than the law, that the court was, in some sense, the law itself, and that the only thing to do was to accept the court and to trust in its processes.",
                "He tried to appeal to influential people, and the influential people were either unwilling or unable to help, and the more he tried, the more he realized that the machinery of the court extended into every corner of the city.",
                "He began to see the court everywhere, in every face, in every conversation, in every glance that seemed to linger a moment too long, and he began to wonder whether the court was not merely a place but a condition.",
                "He began to wonder whether he had always been on trial, whether everyone was always on trial, whether the whole of life was not a trial that had no judge and no verdict and no end.",
                "And the trial continued, and Josef K. continued, and the days passed, and the weeks passed, and the months passed, and the trial did not end, and it would not end, and it could not end.",
                "Josef K. began to understand that it would never end, that he would die before it ended, and that this was the point, that the trial was not meant to end, that the trial was the end.",
                "For the trial was not a trial in the ordinary sense, not a legal proceeding, not an attempt to establish guilt or innocence, but a condition of existence, a way of being in the world, a truth about the human soul.",
                "And Josef K. was not merely a man accused of a crime, but every man, every woman, every human being who has ever lived, who is always accused, who is always judged, who is always waiting for a verdict that never comes.",
                "And the court was not merely a court, but the world itself, the world that judges without reason, that condemns without evidence, that destroys without purpose, the world that is, in the end, the only law that exists.",
                "Josef K. went to his office the next morning, and he sat at his desk, and he looked at the papers before him, and he could not read a single word, and he realized that his mind had been invaded.",
                "The arrest had taken root in him, and it was growing like a cancer that would not stop, and his colleagues noticed that something was wrong, and they asked him if he was ill, and he said that he was fine.",
                "He smiled, and he tried to act as though nothing had happened, and yet he knew that they could see through him, that they could sense the change, that they were beginning to wonder what had become of the man they had known.",
                "He went home that evening, and he sat in his room, and he tried to remember the details of the arrest, the faces of the men, the words they had used, the exact moment when his life had changed.",
                "And he found that he could not remember, that the memory was already beginning to fade, that the whole thing seemed less real than it had been that morning.",
                "And yet he knew that it was real, that it had happened, that the arrest had taken place, and that no amount of forgetting would undo it.",
                "He began to think about the court, about what it might be, about where it might be, about who might be behind it, and the more he thought, the less he understood, and the less he understood, the more afraid he became.",
                "He thought about his childhood, about his parents, about his school days, about his first job, about all the small events that had made up his life, and he could not find any moment, any incident, any choice that might have led to this arrest.",
                "He was, as far as he knew, a man without a past, without a crime, without a reason to be accused, and yet he was accused, and that was that.",
                "He began to understand that the not-knowing was itself the punishment, that the uncertainty was the sentence, that the waiting was the verdict.",
                "And so the first chapter of The Trial ends, and the story continues, and the story will always continue, and Josef K. will always be on trial."
            )),
            EnChapter(2, "The Inquiry", listOf(
                "Josef K. was told that an inquiry would be held, that he should appear at a certain address on a certain day, and that he should bring no papers and no witnesses and no lawyer.",
                "The inquiry was not a trial but a preliminary hearing, and the purpose of the hearing was not to determine his guilt or innocence but simply to establish that the case would proceed.",
                "He arrived at the address, which was in a poor and crowded part of the city, and he found a building that seemed to be a tenement, and he climbed the stairs to the fifth floor.",
                "He entered a room that was hot and airless and full of people, and the room was not a courtroom but a kind of hall, with a low ceiling and rows of wooden benches.",
                "On the benches sat dozens of men and women who had come, like Josef K., to answer for crimes that no one would name.",
                "At the front of the room was a small stage, and on the stage sat a magistrate, an old man with a beard, and beside him sat clerks with pens and papers.",
                "They waited, and everyone waited, and the air was thick with waiting, and Josef K. sat on a bench and looked at the other accused.",
                "He saw in their faces the same confusion that he felt, the same fear, the same not-knowing, and he realized, with a strange kind of relief, that he was not alone.",
                "The magistrate called his name, and Josef K. stood, and the magistrate asked him his profession, his age, his address, and Josef K. answered, and the magistrate wrote something down.",
                "Then the magistrate said that the case would continue, and Josef K. asked what he was accused of, and the magistrate did not answer, and Josef K. asked again.",
                "The magistrate said that he would be informed in due course, and Josef K. said that due course was not a date, and the magistrate said that due course was the only answer that could be given.",
                "Josef K. began to speak, to protest, to explain, to argue, and the magistrate listened in silence, and the clerks wrote, and the other accused watched.",
                "The room grew hotter and hotter, and the words that Josef K. spoke seemed to dissolve in the air, seemed to lose all meaning, seemed to become merely sound.",
                "The magistrate told him that he was speaking too much, that he was making his case worse, that he should trust in the court.",
                "And Josef K. felt the ground shift beneath him, felt the walls close in, felt the whole world become a room that had no door.",
                "He looked at the other accused, and he saw that some of them were smiling, and he did not know whether they were smiling at him or at themselves.",
                "He did not know whether the smile was a kindness or a cruelty, and he did not know whether he should smile back or weep.",
                "The inquiry ended, and Josef K. was told that he would be contacted with the date of the next inquiry, and he was told that he should not discuss the case with anyone.",
                "He was told that he should not attempt to influence the court, and he left the room, and he walked down the stairs, and he went out into the street.",
                "The street was bright and ordinary and full of people, and he felt, for a moment, that the whole thing had been a dream, that none of it was real.",
                "But he knew, in his heart, that it was real, that it was more real than the street, more real than the sun, more real than anything he had ever known.",
                "He went home, and he sat in his room, and he thought about the inquiry, and he thought about the magistrate, and he thought about the other accused.",
                "He thought about the fact that none of them knew what they were accused of either, and he felt a strange kind of kinship with them.",
                "And yet he also felt that he was different, that he was somehow more aware than they were, that he had come to understand something that they did not.",
                "And this understanding, this awareness, was both a gift and a curse, for the gift was that he could see the absurdity of his situation, and the curse was the same.",
                "He went to bed, and he slept, and he dreamed of the court, and the court was not a place but an endless room, and the room was full of people, and the people were all waiting.",
                "And the waiting had no end, and he woke, and he knew that the dream was not a dream, that the dream was the truth, that the dream was what the whole world was.",
                "And he was, whether he liked it or not, a part of it, and so the inquiry ended, and the case continued, and Josef K. continued, and the days passed.",
                "He began to look for the court wherever he went, and he began to see it in the most ordinary places, in the shops, in the offices, in the churches, in the streets.",
                "He began to feel that the court was everywhere and nowhere, that the court was both inside him and outside him, that the court was the world itself.",
                "He began to wonder whether the court was not a court at all, but a condition, a way of being, a truth about the human soul.",
                "He began to wonder whether every human being was not always on trial, and whether every human being was not always waiting for a verdict that would never come.",
                "And in this wondering, he began to find a strange kind of peace, a peace that came from understanding that he was not alone in his not-knowing.",
                "For everyone was in the same not-knowing, everyone was on trial, everyone was waiting, and the waiting was the only thing that was real.",
                "And so the second chapter of The Trial ends, and the story continues, and the story will always continue, and Josef K. will always be on trial."
            )),
            EnChapter(3, "The Lawyer Huld", listOf(
                "Josef K. was told, by someone whose name he did not quite catch, that he should consult a lawyer named Huld, a man who was said to have great influence with the court.",
                "Huld was said to have won many cases, and to be the only hope of anyone who had been accused, and so Josef K. went to see him.",
                "He found Huld old and sick, lying in a great bed in a darkened room, and around him were piled the papers of countless cases.",
                "The papers seemed to have grown into a kind of nest, and the nest seemed to have grown into a kind of tomb, and Huld himself seemed to have become part of the tomb.",
                "Huld listened to Josef K.'s story, and the listening took many hours, and Huld asked questions, and the questions were strange.",
                "The questions did not seem to be about the case but about Josef K. himself, about his past, his habits, his fears, his hopes.",
                "Huld told Josef K. that the case was very difficult, that the case was very serious, that the case would require great patience and great expense.",
                "And Huld told him that the case would require, above all, the willingness of the accused to accept the situation and to do nothing to make it worse.",
                "Josef K. asked Huld what he was accused of, and Huld said that the accusation would become clear in time, and Josef K. asked when the trial would begin.",
                "Huld said that the trial had already begun, and Josef K. asked when the trial would end, and Huld said that no one could know.",
                "No one had ever known, and perhaps the trial would never end, and Josef K. felt the ground beneath him give way.",
                "He felt the walls of the room close in upon him, and he felt the papers around the bed rise up like a wave.",
                "And he felt, for the first time, the true terror of his situation, the terror not of punishment but of uncertainty.",
                "Huld told him that he should be patient, that he should be calm, that he should trust in the court, that he should trust in the lawyer.",
                "And Josef K. listened and said nothing, for he knew, even then, that the process was not something that could be trusted.",
                "The process was something that had to be endured, and Huld told him the story of other cases, other clients, other trials.",
                "And the stories were strange, and the stories were sad, and the stories had no endings, and Josef K. began to understand that his case was not unique.",
                "His case was one of thousands, his case was nothing special, his case was, in fact, quite ordinary, and that, perhaps, was the most terrible thing of all.",
                "His suffering was not exceptional, his suffering was shared by countless others, his suffering was part of a vast and ancient process.",
                "And that process had gone on for centuries and would go on for centuries more, and Huld's maid, a young woman named Leni, came into the room.",
                "She looked at Josef K. with eyes that were both sympathetic and calculating, and she told him that she would help him.",
                "She told him that she would do anything for him, and Josef K. did not know whether to trust her or to fear her.",
                "Leni led him into another room, and she told him things about Huld, and she told him things about the court.",
                "And she told him things that he did not want to know, and she told him these things in a voice that was soft and yet terrible.",
                "Josef K. left Huld's house, and he walked through the streets, and the streets were the same as they had always been, and yet they were not the same.",
                "For he now saw the court everywhere, in every face, in every window, in every shadow, and he knew that he would never again be able to walk through the streets without seeing the court.",
                "And so the days passed, and the weeks passed, and the months passed, and Huld did nothing, and the court did nothing, and the trial did nothing.",
                "And Josef K. waited, and the waiting was the whole of his life, and the waiting was the whole of the punishment.",
                "And he began to understand that the lawyer was not there to help him, that the lawyer was there to prolong the case, to make the case go on forever.",
                "The lawyer was part of the machinery of the court, and the machinery of the court existed not to judge but to consume.",
                "And he began to understand that there was no defense, that there was no appeal, that there was no escape.",
                "The only thing to do was to accept the trial, to accept the waiting, to accept the endless uncertainty, to accept the fact that his life had become nothing more than a case that would never be decided.",
                "And so the third chapter of The Trial ends, and the story continues, and the story will always continue, and Josef K. will always be on trial.",
                "And the trial will never end, and the trial will never end, and the trial will never end."
            )),
            EnChapter(4, "The Cathedral", listOf(
                "Josef K. was asked, one day, to show a visiting Italian businessman around the cathedral of the city, and he agreed.",
                "He was a man of business, and he did not want to offend his superiors, and he did not suspect that the cathedral would be the scene of a revelation.",
                "He arrived at the cathedral early, and the cathedral was vast and dark and empty, and the shadows were deep, and the silence was complete.",
                "Josef K. felt the weight of the centuries press upon him, and he felt, for a moment, a kind of peace.",
                "And then a priest stepped out of the shadows, and the priest called Josef K. by name, and the priest said that he had been waiting for Josef K.",
                "Josef K. felt a chill run down his spine, for he knew that the priest was not an ordinary priest, that the priest was part of the court.",
                "The priest told Josef K. that his case was going badly, that the case was not going as he hoped, that the case might well end in a verdict that Josef K. did not want.",
                "Josef K. asked what he should do, and the priest said that he should stop trying to fight the court.",
                "He should stop trying to understand the court, he should simply accept the court, and Josef K. said that he was innocent.",
                "The priest said that guilt and innocence were not the point, that the point was the trial itself, that the point was the process.",
                "The point was the acceptance of the process, the point was surrender, and the priest then told a parable.",
                "The parable was this: a man comes from the country to seek the law, and he finds a door, and at the door stands a gatekeeper.",
                "The man asks to be admitted, and the gatekeeper says that he cannot be admitted now, but perhaps later, and the man waits.",
                "The man waits for years, and the gatekeeper does not let him in, and the man grows old, and the man grows weak.",
                "And the man asks, as he is dying, why no one else has come to the door in all these years, and the gatekeeper says, this door was made only for you, and now I am going to close it.",
                "Josef K. listened to the parable, and he did not understand it, and he asked the priest what it meant.",
                "The priest said that the parable was about the nature of the law, about the nature of judgment, about the fact that the door of the law is open only to one man.",
                "And that the man can never enter it, and Josef K. argued with the priest, and the argument went on for a long time.",
                "The argument was about the meaning of the parable, and the argument was about the nature of the court, and the argument was about the meaning of life itself.",
                "The priest said that Josef K. was deceiving himself, that Josef K. was refusing to accept the truth, that Josef K. was trying to find meaning in a process that had no meaning.",
                "Josef K. was, in short, refusing to surrender, and Josef K. understood, in that moment, that the priest was right.",
                "He had been deceiving himself, he had been refusing to accept the truth, he had been trying to fight a battle that could not be won.",
                "And the only thing to do was to surrender, and he understood, also, that surrender was not weakness, that surrender was not defeat.",
                "Surrender was, in fact, the only kind of victory that was possible, the only kind of victory that had ever been possible, the only kind of victory that would ever be possible.",
                "The priest disappeared into the shadows, and Josef K. was left alone in the cathedral, and the cathedral was vast and dark and empty.",
                "The shadows were deep, and the silence was complete, and Josef K. knew that he had been changed, that he would never be the same.",
                "He had seen something that could not be unseen, and he walked out of the cathedral, and the streets were the same as they had always been.",
                "And yet they were not the same, for he now saw the court everywhere, and he knew that the court would always be with him.",
                "He knew that the court would always be with everyone, and he knew that there was no escape.",
                "He went home, and he sat in his room, and he thought about the parable of the door of the law, and he thought about the gatekeeper.",
                "He thought about the man from the country, and he thought about the fact that the man had waited his whole life at a door that was meant only for him and that he had never entered.",
                "And he thought about the fact that this was not merely a parable, that this was the truth about his own life, that he had been standing at a door his whole life.",
                "He had never known it, and the door had been made only for him, and perhaps there was no door, and perhaps there was only the waiting, and the waiting was the only thing that was real.",
                "And so the fourth chapter of The Trial ends, and the story continues, and the story will always continue, and Josef K. will always be on trial.",
                "And the trial will never end, and the trial will never end, and the trial will never end."
            )),
            EnChapter(5, "The End", listOf(
                "On the evening before his thirty-first birthday, two men came to Josef K.'s apartment, and the men were dressed in dark coats, and the men were polite.",
                "The men told him that they had been sent to accompany him, and Josef K. knew at once that the end had come.",
                "He did not resist, for he had known for some time that this moment would come, and he had known that resistance was useless.",
                "He had known that the only thing to do was to accept, and he had come, at last, to accept.",
                "The men led him through the streets of the city, and the streets were quiet, and the streets were dark.",
                "The streets were the same streets that Josef K. had walked every day of his life, and yet they were not the same, for he now saw them for the last time.",
                "They walked to the outskirts of the city, to a place where the houses ended and the fields began, and there was a quarry there.",
                "The quarry was small, and the quarry was surrounded by walls, and the walls were made of stone.",
                "The men took Josef K. into the quarry, and they took him to a stone, and they sat him down on the stone.",
                "They held him, and they showed him a knife, and Josef K. understood what was about to happen, and he did not struggle.",
                "He thought about his life, and he thought about his case, and he thought about the court.",
                "He thought about the priest, and he thought about the parable of the door of the law, and he thought about the fact that he had never entered the door.",
                "He thought about the fact that the door had been made only for him, and he thought about the fact that the door was now being closed.",
                "And he thought about the fact that his life had been, in a sense, a trial, and that his death would be, in a sense, a verdict.",
                "And the verdict was that he was guilty, not of any specific crime but of the crime of being alive.",
                "Of the crime of being human, of the crime of existing in a world that was, at bottom, indifferent to his existence.",
                "The men held him, and the men showed him the knife, and the men waited for the knife to be passed, and Josef K. waited too.",
                "The waiting was long, and the waiting was short, and the waiting was, in the end, the whole of his life.",
                "And then the knife was passed, and the knife was used, and Josef K. died, and his last words were, like a dog.",
                "And the words were meant to describe the way he had lived, and the words were meant to describe the way everyone lives.",
                "And the words were meant to describe the way the world treats everyone, for we are all of us, in the end, like dogs.",
                "We are all of us, in the end, killed like dogs, and the killing is not a punishment but a fact.",
                "It is not a judgment but a truth, not an end but a beginning, and the beginning is the beginning of the silence that comes after the trial.",
                "And so the story of The Trial comes to an end, not with a resolution but with a recognition.",
                "Not with a verdict but with a truth, not with a judgment but with a fact.",
                "And the fact is that Josef K. lived, and the fact is that Josef K. died.",
                "And the fact is that Josef K. was everyone, and that everyone is Josef K.",
                "For the trial is not the trial of one man but of all men, and the court is not the court of one city but of all cities.",
                "And the judge is not the judge of one soul but of all souls, and the verdict is not the verdict of one life but of all lives.",
                "And so the story of The Trial ends, not with a death, not with a revelation, not with a resolution, but with a recognition.",
                "The recognition that we are all of us on trial, and that we will never truly know what we are accused of.",
                "And that we will never truly know who is judging us, and that we will never truly know whether the verdict will be guilty or innocent.",
                "And the only thing we can do is to live, and to wait, and to accept, and the waiting is the whole of our lives, and the acceptance is the whole of our courage, and the trial is the whole of our existence, and the trial will never end.",
                "And so the fifth and final chapter of The Trial comes to its close, and the first story of this group comes to its close.",
                "And the second story of this group is yet to be told, and the second story is The Stranger, and The Stranger is the story of a man named Meursault, who lives in the sunlight of Algiers, and who will soon learn what it means to be truly alone."
            ))
        )
